  "window_limited_note": "Window-limited cells never reached the 1/N floor within the horizon; their Shat is a finite-horizon lower bound, so their relative error is NOT a resolved infinite-horizon error. Headline statistics use resolved pairs only.",
  "headline": "On resolved pairs (neither cell window-limited) the absolute finite-horizon SSA stays within a median 10% of its exact-score value for additive score error up to alpha=1.0 x s0 (median 5.2%, p90 13.2%) but only up to +0.1 x var of covariance smoothing (median 8.8%, p90 10.0%), degrading to 12.3% at alpha=2.0 and 21.9% at +0.25 x var; the two families also stratify oppositely at their strongest level - additive error is largest for the high baseline-SSA third (17.2%) whereas smoothing error is largest for the low third (96.9%) - and, critically, absolute SSA degrades EARLIER than the ranking: at covariance smoothing 0.25 the median value error is already 21.9% while Spearman(SSA, MI) is +0.855 against an exact-score +0.877, so ranking stability must not be reported as evidence that the reported SSA magnitude is stable.",
  "headline_additive_only": "On resolved pairs (neither cell window-limited) the absolute finite-horizon SSA stays within a median 10% of its exact-score value for additive score error up to alpha=1.0 x s0 (median 5.2%, p90 13.2%), and degrades beyond that to a median 12.3% (p90 29.0%) at alpha=2.0 x s0, with the high baseline-SSA third worst affected (17.2%); over this range the value error and the ranking loss move together rather than the value degrading first (alpha=1.0: 5.2% value vs 5.1% ranking loss; alpha=2.0: 12.3% value vs 15.6% ranking loss), so on additive evidence alone we claim only that both the reported magnitude and the ordering remain usable up to roughly alpha=1 x s0 and should be treated as approximate beyond it.",
